\subsection{Reasoning Performance}
\textbf{Vehicle Reasoning:} We first We first evaluate the Vehicle Reasoning task (LiDAR and Vision) introduced in in Section~\ref{section:tasks}, as shown in Table~\ref{tab:reasoning_accuracies_categorized}.

For LiDAR reasoning, the Zero-Shot approach achieves moderate accuracy ($47.50\%$–$65.05\%$) across routes, establishing a baseline for detecting sensor misplacement. CoT alone leads to substantial performance drops, suggesting basic sequential reasoning struggles with subtle errors. Adding Self Refine significantly improves accuracy, reaching $72.63\%$ on R2 and $63.89\%$ on R2-right. DriveAgent, however, achieves strong and stable performance, particularly on R2-left ($69.90\%$), demonstrating reliable LiDAR misplacement detection.

For Vision reasoning, detecting misaligned cameras is even more challenging. Zero-Shot and CoT show very low accuracies on left and right views. In contrast, DriveAgent achieves notable gains, including $96.84\%$ accuracy on R2, and clear improvements across left and right variants ($58.25\%$ and $71.30\%$), confirming that modality-specific tuning is crucial for visual sensor reasoning.

% We then evaluate the task 2 introduced in Section~\ref{section:tasks}. For the Lidar-based reasoning task, as shown in Table~\ref{tab:reasoning_accuracies_categorized}, the Zero-Shot approach achieves moderate accuracy levels ranging from 47.50\% to 65.05\% across the datasets and their misplacement variants, establishing a baseline for sensor misplacement detection. The CoT method, without further refinement, results in a notable drop in accuracy, implying that a straightforward sequential reasoning process may not be sufficient to capture the subtle indicators of sensor misplacement. By contrast, augmenting CoT with a Self Refine process substantially improves performance—most notably on the R2 dataset (with overall accuracy of 72.63\% and 63.89\% for the R2-right subset)—demonstrating the benefit of iterative, refined reasoning in identifying Lidar sensor errors. The DriveAgent method further establishes itself as a competitive method by achieving strong performance, particularly on the R2-left subset with an accuracy of 69.90\%, and robustly maintaining performance across other subsets.

% In the vision-based reasoning task, the challenges of detecting camera misplacement are even more pronounced. Both the Zero-Shot and CoT methods display considerably lower accuracy on the misplacement variants, highlighting their limited capability in this domain. In contrast, DriveAgent delivers a marked improvement, attaining a striking accuracy of 96.84\% on the R2 dataset and demonstrating consistent superiority across its left and right variants (58.25\% and 71.30\%, respectively), as well as across the R3 dataset. Such results indicate that a specialized model tuned explicitly for vision data is crucial for effectively compensating for and identifying sensor misplacement under off-nominal conditions. Overall, these findings underscore that while iterative refinement strategies like CoT with Self Refine can enhance Lidar sensor misplacement detection, a dedicated, modality-specific approach—as exemplified by DriveAgent—is essential for achieving high accuracy in identifying misplacements in vision sensors.

