%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Conclusions: Towards evidence-based actions for sustainable development\label{sec:concl}}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

This paper has provided an overview of how various disciplines are connected to different global societal challenges and how these connections have evolved over more than half a century---an area that has previously lacked substantial quantitative evidence.
Specifically, we utilised two types of scores based on specific machine learning algorithms employed in the OpenAlex platform: the field-affinity scores and the SDG-affinity scores.
These scores were used to develop appropriate measures for analysing the organic and dynamic relationships between different disciplines and the SDGs.

Our analysis suggests that since the 2000s, when the MDGs were implemented in earnest, there has been a clear trend towards an increase in publications and/or citations with interdisciplinary content, with many fields showing a tendency to become more interconnected.
The focus on specific global societal issues, as represented by the SDGs, has also highlighted that the interdisciplinarity of these issues and the ways in which they have changed over time are markedly different, reflecting the unique characteristics of each issue.
A considerable body of literature has concentrated, and will continue to concentrate, on the analysis and discussion of each of the SDGs, particularly as we approach the five-year mark before the SDG target deadline.
Within these ongoing discussions, the findings and visualisations presented in this study offer valuable and insightful perspectives from an interdisciplinary and cross-sectoral standpoint.
These insights are especially relevant to policymakers and practitioners as they reflect on the past decades and strategise for the future.

Finally, we would like to address some considerations regarding the methodology employed in this study and outline expectations for future research.
It is essential to continue evaluating and refining the approach used in this study, which applies an Open Bibliometrics-based lens to observe the complex landscape of diverse global societal challenges. 
Open data catalogues of scholarly publication data, including OpenAlex, utilised in this paper, are continually improving in both the quality and quantity of their collections, and these resources will increasingly serve as valuable tools for analysing and visualising global trends.
While commercial databases like Web of Science and Scopus are beneficial for conducting precise analyses from specific perspectives, such open and freely accessible resources \citep{Delgado-Quiros24} can also offer valuable insights into broad trends, particularly for policy-related purposes \citep{Kitajima23,OECDnd,Okamura23,Okamura24,UNESCO23}.
In these contexts, the emphasis is on quick comprehension and a general, albeit rough, understanding.
By recognising the unique characteristics---including both strengths and limitations---of each scholarly database and by using or combining them appropriately for different applications, we can gain new insights and develop more effective lenses through which to examine global trends \citep{Culbert24,Haupka24,Liu23,Visser21}.
Doing so will further enhance the quality and dynamism of discussions surrounding societal impact in both policy and academic spheres, contributing meaningfully to these critical dialogues.


