\subsubsection{Lazy dataset contamination}
Contaminating fresh code samples generated by \stability{} using adversarial datasets from target LLMs is a crucial step in analyzing the performance of $\alg$. To optimize the experiment runtime, we defer this contamination until the execution of $\inbucket$ and $\dkw$.Specifically, we first evaluate the probabilities of the fresh dataset samples coming from all LLMs (\stability{}, \deepseek{}, and \gemma{}). Then, we simulate the contamination using these probability values as proxies for the actual samples. This approach significantly reduces the number of expensive LLM queries. 

For example, suppose we aim to contaminate 10 sample sets $S_1, \ldots, S_{10}$ using an adversarial set $T'$ at a contamination level of $\gamma\%$, generating 3 possible contaminated sets per sample set. A naive approach would require $30|S|$ LLM queries during the experiments. 
In contrast, our deferred contamination method only requires evaluating the probabilities for $\sum_{i=1}^{10}|S_i| + |T|$ samples, leading to substantial query time savings. 




