In the serverless workflow, there is a possibility that many functions need one function's output as input because this function has many successor functions.  When these functions are on the different machine, they have to contend for the network bandwidth. In this experiment, we run a microbenchmark to evaluate the put/get throughput of CouchDB and DStore respectively. We set the key size 64 bytes and the value size  1MB. 
% We run 8 client on one machine and a maximum of 5 machines are used simultaneously , and each machine runs 8clients, for a total of 40 clients
For throughput experiment on CouchDB, we use one machine to run Couchdb and a maximum of 5 machines are used simultaneously and each machine runs 8 clients, for a total of 40 clients to run put/get machine. For DStore get operation, we deploy one machine to run data directory service and one machine to do put operation and a maximum of 5 others machines are used simultaneously and each machine runs 8 clients, for a total of 40 clients to do get operation
The Figure \ref{fig:latencythroughput} c and Figure \ref{fig:latencythroughput} d show the result of put/get throughput on Couchdb and DStore. Observed from the Figure \ref{fig:latencythroughput}c and \ref{fig:latencythroughput}d, the DStore can achieve higher throughput  than Couchdb. For get operation, when there are 24,32,40 clients, DStore can achieve 3.8$\times$, 5.0$\times$, 6.4$\times$ times throughput of CouchDB. For Put operation, when there are 24,32,40 clients,  DStore is about 14.5$\times$, 18.3$\times$, 22.0$\times$ times of Couchdb, this is caused that  the Couchdb has to store the data while the data directory service only store the metadata and the client on the CouchDB has to contends for the limited network bandwidth, which decreases the performance.
